---
import { sortingAlgorithms } from '../../data/sorting';
const {algorithm}=Astro.props;
---
<article class="lesson"><h2>{algorithm.name}: the idea</h2><p><strong>Prerequisites:</strong> {algorithm.prerequisites}.</p><p>{algorithm.detail}</p><h3>Try it on the same input</h3><p>{algorithm.experiment}</p><h3>Time, memory, and stability</h3><p>n is the number of input items. {algorithm.complexity}</p><p>A stable sort preserves the original order of items whose values are equal. This implementation is {algorithm.stable?'stable':'not stable'}. Identity labels record each item’s initial position. An unstable algorithm can preserve equal-item order on a particular input; it makes no guarantee for all inputs.</p><p>These bounds describe the sorting algorithm itself. The visualization saves a copy of the array at every operation for reversible playback, so recording uses additional time and memory. Its input size is capped at 24 items.</p><h3>When to use it—and what to watch</h3><p>{algorithm.limitation}</p><h3>Reading the display</h3><p>Comparisons count tests between item values. A swap writes two array positions; a merge write updates one. The counters exclude copying merge buffers, index tests, and playback work, so they are teaching measurements rather than elapsed-time benchmarks. Green marks final positions; a highlighted range indicates current work. For recursive algorithms, depth counts the active call levels.</p><h3>Continue exploring</h3><ul>{sortingAlgorithms.filter(a=>a.slug!==algorithm.slug).map(a=><li><a href={`/algorithms/sorting/${a.slug}/`}>{a.name}</a> — {a.summary}</li>)}</ul><h3>Sources and further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/21elementary/">Sedgewick and Wayne: elementary sorts</a></li><li><a href="https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/22mergesort/">Sedgewick and Wayne: mergesort</a></li><li><a href="https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/23quicksort/">Sedgewick and Wayne: quicksort</a></li><li><a href="https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/cheatsheet/">Algorithms, 4th Edition: sorting properties</a></li></ul></article>
<style>.lesson{max-width:780px;margin:3rem auto;line-height:1.85}.lesson h2{font-size:1.8rem}.lesson h3{margin-top:2rem;font-size:1.25rem}.lesson li{margin:.6rem 0}</style>
